# Vanadium Flow Battery Power & Cooling System for Data Centers

## 1. Problem Statement
* **AI & HPC Energy Demands:** Massive energy consumption driven by high-density AI workloads and high-performance computing.
* **Supply Chain & Thermal Bottlenecks:** Global shortage of copper transformers paired with intense localized heat generated within facility racks.
* **Single-Purpose Core Inefficiencies:** Legacy infrastructure relies on single-purpose systems that fail to make dual use of the core energy and cooling transport media.

---

## 2. Proposed Solution
* An integrated **Electrochemical Power and Cooling System (EPCS)** that leverages Vanadium Redox Flow Battery (VRFB) chemistry—acting as "electronic blood" to deliver both power storage and direct liquid cooling simultaneously.

---

## 3. Key Accomplishments & Benefits
* **Dual-Use Efficiency:** Maximizes system core utility by unifying thermal management and power delivery into a single liquid flow loop.
* **PUE Improvement:** Substantially lowers overall facility energy consumption by bypassing traditional, energy-intensive mechanical chillers.
* **Direct Heat Dissipation:** Uses circulating liquid electrolyte to capture and dissipate heat directly from high-density server racks.
* **Grid Resilience & LDES:** Provides grid-decoupled long-duration backup power without diesel emissions or heavy reliance on transformers.
* **Enhanced Safety:** Utilizes a non-flammable, aqueous electrolyte ideal for indoor and critical infrastructure deployment.
